---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const outcomes = [
  { metric: '55%', label: 'reduction in repeat customer escalations through prioritized remediation' },
  { metric: '99.9%', label: 'service availability / SLA protection across customer-facing SaaS environments' },
  { metric: '40%', label: 'reduction in repeat incidents through root-cause and readiness work' },
  { metric: '30%', label: 'improvement in release reliability through stronger readiness practices' },
];

const remediationLoop = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'See the customer signal',
    body: 'Treat recurring escalations and service-impact patterns as evidence of a systemic problem, not just isolated operational events.',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    title: 'Find the pattern',
    body: 'Use production behavior, repeat-incident history, and root-cause work to separate one-off noise from issues worth sustained remediation.',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    title: 'Create ownership',
    body: 'Coordinate across Product, Engineering, and customer-facing teams so recurring issues have clear priority, ownership, and follow-through.',
  },
  {
    number: '04',
    title: 'Protect the next release',
    body: 'Carry lessons into release readiness, operating controls, and response expectations so fixes reduce recurrence rather than end with one incident.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Reliability & Cross-Functional Remediation | Mike Gilmore"
  description="A public-safe case study of recurring SaaS issue remediation, customer escalation reduction, production readiness, and cross-functional reliability work."
>
  <header class="case-hero shell">
    <div class="case-hero__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case study · Reliability & customer impact</p>
      <h1>Turning recurring SaaS issues into owned, cross-functional remediation.</h1>
      <p>
        Reliability work created the most value when production patterns could be connected to customer impact, assigned
        clear ownership, and carried through remediation and release readiness instead of being treated as isolated incidents.
      </p>
      <p class="case-note">Public-safe summary based on substantiated professional experience. Incident details, customer identities, and internal production artifacts are intentionally excluded.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="case-hero__role" aria-label="Role and focus">
      <p class="signal-label">Role pattern</p>
      <p>Site reliability with customer-impact and delivery context</p>
      <p class="signal-label">Focus</p>
      <p>Pattern analysis · prioritization · remediation · release readiness</p>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <section class="case-outcomes" aria-labelledby="reliability-outcomes-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="case-section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Evidence</p>
        <h2 id="reliability-outcomes-heading">The goal was fewer repeat problems, not faster ticket closure.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric-grid">
        {outcomes.map((outcome) => (
          <div class="case-metric">
            <strong>{outcome.metric}</strong>
            <span>{outcome.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-story" aria-labelledby="reliability-context-heading">
    <aside class="case-story__rail">
      <p class="eyebrow">Context</p>
      <p>Customer-facing SaaS</p>
      <p>Product + Engineering + service teams</p>
      <p>2022–2026 experience</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="case-story__body">
      <h2 id="reliability-context-heading">A production incident can end while the customer problem remains.</h2>
      <p>
        Customer-facing SaaS reliability created two kinds of work at once: restoring service when something went wrong,
        and identifying which recurring patterns needed deeper corrective action so they did not keep returning as escalations.
      </p>
      <p>
        That required working across team boundaries. Product and Engineering needed enough context to prioritize systemic
        fixes, customer-facing teams needed clearer expectations, and operational practices needed to carry lessons into
        future releases and response behavior.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft" aria-labelledby="remediation-loop-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="case-section-heading case-section-heading--dark">
        <p class="eyebrow">Remediation loop</p>
        <h2 id="remediation-loop-heading">Move from production signal to durable corrective action.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="case-flow">
        {remediationLoop.map((step) => (
          <li>
            <span class="case-flow__number">{step.number}</span>
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <p>{step.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-story" aria-labelledby="reliability-judgment-heading">
    <aside class="case-story__rail">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this demonstrates</p>
      <p>Root-cause analysis</p>
      <p>Cross-functional prioritization</p>
      <p>Production governance</p>
      <p>Customer-impact analysis</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="case-story__body">
      <h2 id="reliability-judgment-heading">The important boundary was between technical evidence and organizational action.</h2>
      <p>
        Observability and production context helped identify what was happening, but the harder part was often translating
        that evidence into an owned decision: which pattern mattered, who needed to act, how readiness should change, and
        how customer-facing teams should understand the risk.
      </p>
      <p>
        That operating model helped reduce repeat customer escalations by 55%, repeat incidents by 40%, and improve release
        reliability by 30% while protecting 99.9% service availability / SLA performance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark" aria-labelledby="reliability-takeaway-heading">
    <div class="shell case-takeaway">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Takeaway</p>
      <h2 id="reliability-takeaway-heading">Reliability becomes customer value when recurring technical signals become clear ownership and fewer repeat failures.</h2>
      <p>
        The pattern I carry forward is to connect technical evidence to customer impact, prioritize recurrence rather than
        noise, and make sure remediation changes the next operating or release decision instead of ending with the incident.
      </p>
      <a class="button button--secondary case-button-light" href="/work/">Back to selected work</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
